
<p>Charlie Warzel, writing for The Atlantic (gift link):</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>It is curious, then, that, as news came out that the Nvidia CEO,
Jensen Huang, had had a romantic relationship with a Chinese
businesswoman who was also an asset of Beijing’s Ministry of State
Security, notable factions of Silicon Valley and Washington, D.C.,
seemed nonplussed. “Even incredible humans make mistakes,” Bill
Ackman, the billionaire hedge-fund manager, <a href="https://x.com/BillAckman/status/2119535982999748830">posted</a> on X
yesterday. “We should all support him and his family. Onward.” The
venture capitalist Shaun Maguire <a href="https://x.com/shaunmmaguire/status/2119560017624158265">offered</a> a five-point
analysis of Huang’s situation, noting that the CEO did something
“incredibly dumb” and “bad for America,” but that “my guess is
there was no bad intention.” He then argued, “I believe Jensen is
irreplaceable for America’s AI leadership.” Elon Musk agreed. From
there, the posts, written before we know all the facts of the
scandal, got only more <a href="https://x.com/nikitabier/status/2119546515404554613?a=f64k7FpICXhH7V1BfLc&amp;s=20">absurd</a>, including
<a href="https://x.com/techleadhd/status/2119574905159286985">defending</a> Huang’s affair on the grounds that his kids are
adults now. [...]</p>

<p>Huang’s thinking on issues such as AI doomerism (he’s anti-doom)
and chip allocation in China (he’s for it — a position that
warrants examining, given this weekend’s revelations) influences
U.S. policy. This <a href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DfhRrVWinGI/?obrf=MTI0OGtuc2V4YW1pdA==">centrality</a> is why one tech analyst on
CNBC this morning implied that replacing Huang was a comical
suggestion. Huang is a singular figure: the linchpin of the
company that is arguably the linchpin of the American economy.
Framed another way, Nvidia has much of the financial system in a
hostage situation. Huang is too big to fail. Which may be why so
many people seem motivated to downplay or protect him right now.</p>
</blockquote>

